WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.730
Film Courage: When you walk into an audition,
what are you telling yourself?

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:07.149
Even if you think “I have this idea about
who this character is and I don’t think

00:00:07.149 --> 00:00:13.000
I’m going to get it [the role], I’m going
to still show up and try to do what I can.”

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Chad Lindberg: What do I tell myself?

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.770
Film Courage: Are you giving yourself any
kind of a talk or no?

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.620
Each situation is totally different?

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:22.930
Chad Lindberg: I don’t think for me talks
really help in that instance.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:28.689
You know, if I have an audition that day,
you’re thinking okay, especially if it’s

00:00:28.689 --> 00:00:33.290
a 5:00 p.m. because I hate later auditions
because you have to hold onto that all day.

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:35.100
It drives me crazy.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:40.180
I like a good, early audition in the morning,
let’s get it done, you know?

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:41.740
Because it’s tough.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:45.730
You’ve got to hang onto that all day.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:48.850
I think preparation is key.

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:53.730
A talk for me won’t do it because I’m
so nervous and I’m like “Shut up, Chad.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:57.010
I don’t want to hear you babbling right
now.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:58.150
Just go on and do it.”

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:03.430
And I think you’ve got to just go in there
and do it, you’ve got to be prepared.

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:06.350
I like to have my sides here.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:11.350
I will memorize the whole entire thing, especially
if it’s the next day.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:16.500
And one thing I do to sort of help that…Actors,
pay attention!

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.740
It’s really a great thing.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.000
Every phone now has a voice recorder, right?

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:26.930
So I will take the sides and I’ll read every
line even if it’s not my line, I will read

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:32.120
the entire scene out loud at a very monotone
voice and then I’ll replay that over and

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:33.890
over and over again.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:35.710
And you go in the next day and it will be
memorized.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:41.110
So I think preparation will take away sort
of any anxiety that you…well, you should

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:47.640
be anxious and nervous but it’s going to
take away that extra moment of fear because,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:55.140
look we’ve all…actors, sometimes we don’t
prepare as much as we should.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:59.100
We’ve gone in…we’ve all been there…we’ve
all been there.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.659
And you get called on it.

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:05.030
You’ll go in there and you’ll be doing
your thing and you lose your place and you’ve

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:07.920
got to go back, it’s awful!

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.640
Your face turns red and you know in that instance
“I’m not getting this.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.060
This just went out the door.”

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:18.540
So you’ve got to be prepared and it’s
tough to do because sometimes you’ll get

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:22.480
an audition for the next day and you’ll
get another one and another one, you’ve

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:27.110
got three the next day and they’re all 15
pages long and they want to see every scene,

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:30.760
so you’ve got to be willing to do that work.

